phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:5:Drive Phase 1 of autofdtd from an almost-empty repository to a credible, pip-installable, GPU-first FDTD package whose feature plan is anchored explicitly to the local Tidy3D EM API surface. The loop must produce a Tidy3D-inspired public API, a tagged execution IR, a scene-compilation pipeline, a chunk-based multi-node-ready runtime, Warp kernel conventions, concrete implementations for the highest-priority Tidy3D feature families, validation examples, and documentation without copying protected code or docs from any reference project.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:32:- Separate scene modeling, scene compilation, runtime stepping, and IO or packaging into distinct subsystems.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:72:Scheduling findings from `papers/systolic_fdtd.pdf`: a naive GPU FDTD is constrained mostly by memory traffic and synchronization, so the important architectural seam is the split between chunk-local interior work, lower-dimensional boundary exchange, and a pluggable execution policy that may later delay or overlap neighbor consumption. Phase 1 should remain bulk-synchronous by default, but chunk metadata and runtime orchestration should already preserve per-face, per-stage halo semantics, interior-versus-boundary planning, and scheduler hooks so future systolic or overlapped execution can be added without redesign.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:80:Working-memory seed facts that later tasks should preserve: chunk is the architectural unit while rank or device is placement; stored topology and logical topology diverge because of symmetry and periodic or Bloch remapping; scene compilation must remain distinct from stepping; continuous problem specifications must lower through explicit interpolation and restriction into discrete execution artifacts; geometry lowering and materialization must precede serious kernels; subpixel smoothing can induce effective anisotropy even for physically isotropic materials; stage-specific halos and monitor metadata should be explicit; Tidy3D-style compatibility should be represented as a tagged immutable IR with normalization rules and unsupported-feature policy; runtime metrics should include Gcells/s plus separate end-to-end and steady-state timing; and near-to-far should be built on top of DFT monitor infrastructure rather than as an isolated feature.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:92:  - logging and convergence behavior are first-class runtime deliverables, not incidental byproducts
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:93:  - kernel/runtime support must be explicit for materials, boundaries, sources, monitors, and postprocessing rather than implied by API support
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:135:- `src/autofdtd/materials/dispersive.py` now provides a Phase 1 `PoleResidue` public model with JSON-safe complex-pair storage, causal validation (`Re(a) <= 0`), `eps_model()`, and limited `Medium` conversion helpers.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:139:- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `Sellmeier` into a typed `SellmeierIR` while keeping runtime compilation on the shared pole-residue auxiliary-state contract.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:140:- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `Lorentz`, `Drude`, and `Debye` into typed IR families while runtime compilation still targets the shared pole-residue auxiliary-state contract.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:143:- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles `Lorentz`, `Drude`, and `Debye` by first reducing them to equivalent `PoleResidue` terms, re-tagging the compiled coefficients with the public family name, and failing explicitly when converted poles are too stiff for the current exact-hold timestep path.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:148:- Reserve subsystem namespaces now: `api`, `core`, `geometry`, `materials`, `boundaries`, `sources`, `monitors`, `grid`, `modes`, `ir`, `compiler`, `runtime`, `kernels`, `diagnostics`, `examples`.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:149:- Use `hatchling` with a `src/` layout and ship a small CLI (`autofdtd-feature-matrix`) so the scaffold has a concrete install/runtime smoke target before solver code exists.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:159:- Keep `GeometryArray` as a public wrapper only; repeated placements lower into ordinary grouped and transformed instances so runtime/compiler code can treat arrays as pre-expanded ordered geometry.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:166:- Treat PEC and PMC as explicit constitutive clamp modes in coefficient compilation and runtime helpers instead of pretending they are extremely lossy ordinary media.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:168:- Phase 1 PoleResidue runtime support is electric-only and uses one complex polarization accumulator per pole; magnetic dispersion remains out of scope until later material families require it.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:183:- Later runtime and chunk-planning tasks can treat `IsotropicMaterialCoefficients` plus `ConstitutiveMode` as the stable baseline constitutive contract before dispersive and anisotropic state is added.
phase1/runs/task-017-attempt-011/prompt.txt:184:- Later Lorentz, Drude, Debye, and Sellmeier tasks should lower onto the `PoleResidueMaterialCoefficients` auxiliary-state contract whenever the family can be reduced to pole-residue terms instead of creating parallel runtime state layouts.
